	urldate = {2024-01-29},
	abstract = {Voices of Country, Ars Electronica 2020, 360º   We acknowledge the traditional custodians of the land on which this movie was shot, the land of the Dja Dja Wurrung of the Kulin Nations. We pay our respects to their elders -- past, present and emerging -- and to all Indigenous and Torres Straits Islanders witnessing this movie.   `Voices of Country' is a story which respectfully gives a small insight into the rich and ancient culture of this continent, Australia, while reflecting and critiquing the colonial past, and projecting an outlook into a utopian future.     Please watch in high resolution!     Credits: Director, Idea and Concept: Rochus Urban Hinkel   Text and Reading: H{\'e}l{\`e}ne Frichot   Scans and Renders: Melissa Irehata and Tony Yu, MSD NExT Lab   Production: Lia Bach with Michael Fragstein \& Christian Schlaeffer, B{\"u}ro Achter April   Natural Sounds: Ashton Dickerson, Urban Light Lab; Rochus Urban Hinkel   Sound Design: Michael Fragstein, B{\"u}ro Achter April   A project by Rochus Urban Hinkel, Melbourne School of Design, at The University of Melbourne, Melbourne, Australia. 2020.   We thank Mike Lelliot and Cath McKinney for their support and the opportunity to spend time on the land where they live for this project.     Important References we drew on include:   Brenna Bhanda, Colonial Lives of Property: Law, Land, and Racial Regimes of Ownership, Durgham, DC: Duke University Press, 2018.   Karen Burns, "Frontier conflict, contact, exchange: re-imagining colonial architecture", Imagining... Proceedings of the 27th International SAHANZ Conference, 30 June to 2 July, Society of Architectural Historians, Australia and New Zealand (SAHANZ), 2012, pp. 70--80.   Bill Gammage, The Biggest Estate on Earth, Crows Nest, NSW: Allen and Unwin, 2011.   Lovell and Chen Architects (Emma Hewitt, Dr Conrad Hamann, Anita Brady), Thematic Environmental History, Aboriginal History, Report prepared for the City of Greater Bendigo, 2013.   Paul Memmott,